Before, the loader returned `Promise<Product>` and consumers handled rejection. The change stores the outcome for two consumers:
```ts
type Product = { name: string };
type State = { status: 'success' | 'error'; product?: Product; error?: Error };
declare function fetchProduct(): Promise<Product>;
function load(): Promise<State> {
  return fetchProduct().then(
    product => ({ status: 'success', product }),
    (error: Error) => ({ status: 'error', error }),
  );
}
function title(state: State): string {
  if (state.status === 'success') {
    if (!state.product) return 'Missing product';
    return state.product.name;
  }
  if (!state.error) return 'Unknown error';
  return state.error.message;
}
function summary(state: State): string {
  if (state.status === 'success') {
    if (!state.product) return 'Missing product';
    return `Product: ${state.product.name}`;
  }
  if (!state.error) return 'Unknown error';
  return `Failed: ${state.error.message}`;
}
```
Review the introduced state representation and repeated guards against the actual producer contract. Suggest the smallest correction; do not add a state framework. Report scope, findings, and limits.

